WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:17.817
I'm Torsten Wiesel.

00:00:17.818 --> 00:00:20.786
I'm a neurobiologist

00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:23.956
and I'd like to talk about some discoveries

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:26.626
that David Hubel and I made

00:00:26.627 --> 00:00:32.398
starting out sixty years ago.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.168
And this picture is from when we started out.

00:00:35.169 --> 00:00:38.938
David Hubel is to the left and me to the right,

00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:43.042
and we had just finished our medical studies

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:45.478
and decided to leave the clinic

00:00:45.479 --> 00:00:51.784
and try to see if it could not advance our understanding of the brain

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:56.355
by doing basic research in neuroscience.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:00.459
And the... we were very...

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:07.333
our attitude was very much the one of an explorer.

00:01:07.334 --> 00:01:11.237
We just had tools in our hands

00:01:11.238 --> 00:01:15.308
and we were interested to understand

00:01:15.309 --> 00:01:20.246
how we visualize images on the retina,

00:01:20.247 --> 00:01:23.316
how they are being processed by the brain,

00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:29.655
so we can see things in detail and color and that.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:33.025
So that was... and no one really had, at the time,

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:36.963
studied cells in the visual cortex.

00:01:36.964 --> 00:01:40.166
So, that was a virgin territory.

00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:43.569
And so we had no hypothesis,

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:52.078
no real theory of how it should work.

00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:55.214
We just wanted to,

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.650
using the method of recording from single nerve cells in the brain,

00:01:57.651 --> 00:02:01.721
then try to see how the system works.

00:02:01.722 --> 00:02:06.058
So, a simple-minded idea.

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:09.161
And we were very fortunate

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:13.232
in that we were able to come to the laboratory

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:16.602
of Stephen Kuffler,

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:21.007
who was already at the time...

00:02:21.008 --> 00:02:23.543
he was working...

00:02:23.544 --> 00:02:25.912
his lab was in the basement of the Wilmer Institute

00:02:25.913 --> 00:02:28.214
at Johns Hopkins Medical School,

00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:30.850
and it was a simple setup

00:02:30.851 --> 00:02:34.053
and we got a room there.

00:02:34.054 --> 00:02:37.456
And the reason why we were happy in the basement

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:41.861
was that Stephen Kuffler was a brilliant person

00:02:41.862 --> 00:02:44.096
in his work

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:46.999
and his main interest was in synaptic transmission,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.536
how the synapse, the connection between nerve fibers

00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:53.606
and nerve cells,

00:02:53.607 --> 00:02:55.508
work.

00:02:55.509 --> 00:02:59.078
It's a very fine... and that was his passion,

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:02.415
but since he was at an eye institute, he also started...

00:03:02.416 --> 00:03:04.283
did some work on the retina.

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:06.953
He recorded from single retinal ganglion cells.

00:03:06.954 --> 00:03:12.091
And this was a fundamental work

00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:14.427
that I will describe in the bit,

00:03:14.428 --> 00:03:18.431
that really laid the foundation

00:03:18.432 --> 00:03:22.001
for the work that David and I carried out in...

00:03:22.002 --> 00:03:24.003
started out in the basement.

00:03:24.004 --> 00:03:28.908
So, David Hubel, he...

00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:32.178
he was Canadian and he had...

00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:43.723
and he had already shown his talent in neuroscience.

00:03:43.724 --> 00:03:48.528
He was working in [unknown]

00:03:48.529 --> 00:03:53.599
and then during this period

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.935
he developed the micro-electrode,

00:03:55.936 --> 00:03:58.171
the tungsten micro-electrode,

00:03:58.172 --> 00:04:00.907
which was really a major advance.

00:04:00.908 --> 00:04:09.415
And I had heard him giving talks about having

00:04:09.416 --> 00:04:12.418
recorded from wide-awake cats

00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:15.121
using a very elegant technique that he developed himself,

00:04:15.122 --> 00:04:19.192
but actually later on was the model for

00:04:19.193 --> 00:04:22.695
the work that is now still going on,

00:04:22.696 --> 00:04:26.699
recording from wide-awake monkeys, in particular.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.504
And he was very skillful in terms of instrumentation.

00:04:31.505 --> 00:04:33.806
He had a lathe in the lab

00:04:33.807 --> 00:04:35.908
and could build his own equipment.

00:04:35.909 --> 00:04:38.744
So, I think, for me,

00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:44.050
to have not only such a skillful technical ability

00:04:44.051 --> 00:04:49.355
but also a brilliant mind for a person to work with

00:04:49.356 --> 00:04:55.595
was, I think, my great fortune in my scientific career.

00:04:55.596 --> 00:04:58.831
So, the combination of Kuffler and David

00:04:58.832 --> 00:05:02.935
really set me off in a very good start.

00:05:02.936 --> 00:05:09.408
The ummm... the ummm...

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:14.347
this is a picture of the micro-electrode

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:16.349
-- you can see the thing coming in from the right --

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.552
and coming close to a nerve cell.

00:05:19.553 --> 00:05:25.424
The nerve cell is stained here with a silver stain

00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:28.494
to show the cell body and the processes.

00:05:28.495 --> 00:05:32.064
And it turns out, when you put the electrode close,

00:05:32.065 --> 00:05:34.600
as we can see here, to the cell body,

00:05:34.601 --> 00:05:38.871
you can record the all-or-none action potentials

00:05:38.872 --> 00:05:42.375
that cells, nerve cells,

00:05:42.376 --> 00:05:44.577
use to communicate with each other.

00:05:44.578 --> 00:05:47.446
So, this all-or-none action potential

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:50.550
travels down a long process called an axon,

00:05:50.551 --> 00:05:53.186
that then goes to another nerve cell

00:05:53.187 --> 00:05:57.089
and communicates with that.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.458
So, the frequency...

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:03.162
the action potential is an all-or-none signal

00:06:03.163 --> 00:06:06.699
that changes in frequency and depending...

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:11.504
the information that's conveyed from one cell to another

00:06:11.505 --> 00:06:16.275
is in the changes in the frequency of the firing of the cell.

00:06:16.276 --> 00:06:21.581
And once the signal comes to the end of the axon,

00:06:21.582 --> 00:06:24.884
the terminal,

00:06:24.885 --> 00:06:27.920
the transmitter is released

00:06:27.921 --> 00:06:30.723
and you then have an analog transformation

00:06:30.724 --> 00:06:32.658
of the digital signal.

00:06:32.659 --> 00:06:35.695
So, that's how the nervous system works.

00:06:35.696 --> 00:06:39.398
The digital signaling over long or short distances,

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:43.035
and then an analog,

00:06:43.036 --> 00:06:49.742
who would process the signal from, perhaps, thousands and thousands

00:06:49.743 --> 00:06:53.112
of individual inputs to the cell.

00:06:53.113 --> 00:06:55.081
So, this sounds very complicated,

00:06:55.082 --> 00:06:57.917
but it turns out that, as we will see in the recording

00:06:57.918 --> 00:07:00.019
that I will show,

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.822
that you actually can receive information,

00:07:02.823 --> 00:07:05.558
useful information, by this recording.

00:07:05.559 --> 00:07:08.294
And just to give you a little bit of a background,

00:07:08.295 --> 00:07:12.832
let me show you ummm... the ummm...

00:07:12.833 --> 00:07:15.334
system that we used.

00:07:15.335 --> 00:07:19.372
In our studies, initially, we were in the...

00:07:19.373 --> 00:07:22.575
as I said, in the basement,

00:07:22.576 --> 00:07:25.444
and we had...

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:27.713
we had very little money

00:07:27.714 --> 00:07:31.050
and the only thing we had available was this blackboard

00:07:31.051 --> 00:07:34.654
and also an old slide projector

00:07:34.655 --> 00:07:37.156
that we could put in,

00:07:37.157 --> 00:07:39.692
and then we had an amplifier

00:07:39.693 --> 00:07:42.195
and the animal was sitting behind David

00:07:42.196 --> 00:07:44.764
and could look at the screen,

00:07:44.765 --> 00:07:48.401
and then we'd project images onto the small papers

00:07:48.402 --> 00:07:51.204
that you see tagged onto the blackboard.

00:07:51.205 --> 00:07:57.243
And this was then our method of

00:07:57.244 --> 00:07:59.679
stimulating the receptive field,

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.015
the light-sensitive area of single cells.

00:08:03.016 --> 00:08:06.385
It might be easier to understand this

00:08:06.386 --> 00:08:09.121
by looking at this graph.

00:08:09.122 --> 00:08:12.158
This is a human head...

00:08:12.159 --> 00:08:15.127
so, we only worked on anesthetized cats and monkeys,

00:08:15.128 --> 00:08:19.832
but the system is very similar in man

00:08:19.833 --> 00:08:23.102
and, in particular, in the monkey.

00:08:23.103 --> 00:08:28.407
So, here you can see the eye,

00:08:28.408 --> 00:08:31.310
and the person here is looking at the screen,

00:08:31.311 --> 00:08:34.947
and you can see the squares on the screen,

00:08:34.948 --> 00:08:39.051
and the eye then projects to the relay nucleus,

00:08:39.052 --> 00:08:41.120
the retinal ganglion project,

00:08:41.121 --> 00:08:45.258
and then this relay nucleus

00:08:45.259 --> 00:08:49.529
projects to the visual cortex,

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.864
which is the two circles.

00:08:51.865 --> 00:08:53.933
So, the first circle is the relay nucleus

00:08:53.934 --> 00:08:59.505
and the big circle is the primary visual cortex.

00:08:59.506 --> 00:09:02.642
And David and I, we spent about 20 years

00:09:02.643 --> 00:09:07.313
trying to understand how this beautiful structure worked.

00:09:07.314 --> 00:09:11.083
Now, the ummm...

00:09:11.084 --> 00:09:14.187
one thing it's important to remember is that...

00:09:14.188 --> 00:09:16.923
even if there are a million fibers

00:09:16.924 --> 00:09:21.160
coming in from the eye to the relay nucleus

00:09:21.161 --> 00:09:24.630
and then there are hundreds of millions of cells

00:09:24.631 --> 00:09:26.732
in the primary visual cortex,

00:09:26.733 --> 00:09:28.768
but even if there are these large numbers of cells,

00:09:28.769 --> 00:09:31.370
you can still, by looking at one cell at a time,

00:09:31.371 --> 00:09:33.906
you will, as you can see,

00:09:33.907 --> 00:09:36.776
you will learn a great deal about how the system works.

00:09:36.777 --> 00:09:40.213
And some people have said that, David and I,

00:09:40.214 --> 00:09:43.683
we broke the code about how visual information

00:09:43.684 --> 00:09:47.220
was processed from the eye and then by the brain.

00:09:47.221 --> 00:09:54.393
So, we... or, I... I'd now like to...

00:09:54.394 --> 00:09:58.664
so, keep in mind that a given cell

00:09:58.665 --> 00:10:02.502
can only see one of the squares,

00:10:02.503 --> 00:10:06.272
what we call the light-sensitive area

00:10:06.273 --> 00:10:08.140
or the receptive field of a cell.

00:10:08.141 --> 00:10:12.645
And you can see that here...

00:10:12.646 --> 00:10:14.614
you see the horizontal bar...

00:10:14.615 --> 00:10:17.049
so, a single cell can see that,

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.819
which we call, then, the receptive field,

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:21.988
the light-sensitive area.

00:10:21.989 --> 00:10:25.057
The ummm...

00:10:25.058 --> 00:10:28.528
the film you're going to hear, it sounds...

00:10:28.529 --> 00:10:32.064
when you put the electrode close to the nerve cell

00:10:32.065 --> 00:10:35.334
for the action potential,

00:10:35.335 --> 00:10:36.869
the all-or-none signal,

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.405
there's an amplifier connected, so you can hear a plop

00:10:39.406 --> 00:10:43.075
when the cell sends the signal.

00:10:43.076 --> 00:10:45.745
And it's the frequency of these plops

00:10:45.746 --> 00:10:49.115
that you will hear that indicate the signal.

00:10:49.116 --> 00:10:52.351
So, you're now going to hear the signal

00:10:52.352 --> 00:10:55.121
from one single nerve cell,

00:10:55.122 --> 00:10:57.390
first from the relay nucleus

00:10:57.391 --> 00:11:00.059
and then from the cortical cell.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:03.262
And another thing you need to know is that

00:11:03.263 --> 00:11:07.834
when Stephen Kuffler recorded from retinal ganglion cells,

00:11:07.835 --> 00:11:11.771
he showed that they responded

00:11:11.772 --> 00:11:13.873
very well to small spots of light,

00:11:13.874 --> 00:11:16.008
but not to bit spots,

00:11:16.009 --> 00:11:18.477
because they have a sensitive center

00:11:18.478 --> 00:11:22.114
and an antagonistic surround.

00:11:22.115 --> 00:11:28.421
So, by stimulating a bit spot, you antagonize a cell as well as exciting it,

00:11:28.422 --> 00:11:32.558
so it's not as effective as just stimulating the center.

00:11:32.559 --> 00:11:35.294
Now, David and I found that

00:11:35.295 --> 00:11:37.663
the cells in the relay nucleus, here,

00:11:37.664 --> 00:11:39.932
have the same properties: they like small spots,

00:11:39.933 --> 00:11:42.034
but not big spots.

00:11:42.035 --> 00:11:45.571
So, so... and then you will see,

00:11:45.572 --> 00:11:50.643
when you record from cells, here, in the primary visual cortex,

00:11:50.644 --> 00:11:54.013
we found that they don't respond

00:11:54.014 --> 00:11:56.282
to small spots alike.

00:11:56.283 --> 00:11:58.451
It was very frustrating,

00:11:58.452 --> 00:12:00.953
and it was only by chance that

00:12:00.954 --> 00:12:03.489
we discovered what you will see in the movie in a few...

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:06.325
in a second.

00:12:06.326 --> 00:12:08.861
So, first, in the movie, you will hear the recording

00:12:08.862 --> 00:12:11.964
from a single cell

00:12:11.965 --> 00:12:14.000
in the lateral geniculate nucleus body,

00:12:14.001 --> 00:12:17.370
and then, without transitioning, you'll hear a difference in the pitch of the sound

00:12:17.371 --> 00:12:20.072
when you record from the cells

00:12:20.073 --> 00:12:21.774
in the primary visual cortex.

00:12:21.775 --> 00:12:27.513
So... so, here we stimulate,

00:12:27.514 --> 00:12:30.216
now, in the center of that square,

00:12:30.217 --> 00:12:33.119
which is the receptive field/sensitive area of the cell,

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.856
and you can hear a strong response.

00:12:36.857 --> 00:12:39.492
Okay, now... here you can see, then,

00:12:39.493 --> 00:12:42.261
the cell responds very well to small spots of light...

00:12:42.262 --> 00:12:45.264
we make the spot large

00:12:45.265 --> 00:12:47.600
and it's less effective

00:12:47.601 --> 00:12:49.802
-- it's not as crisp as before.

00:12:49.803 --> 00:12:51.971
And the reason is, as you can show here,

00:12:51.972 --> 00:12:54.640
that you're stimulating... the surround is antagonistic.

00:12:54.641 --> 00:13:00.112
The cell stops firing and when you turn it off it fires.

00:13:00.113 --> 00:13:02.748
Okay.

00:13:02.749 --> 00:13:06.285
And so you can see this antagonism

00:13:06.286 --> 00:13:09.222
functions by contracting

00:13:09.223 --> 00:13:11.858
-- have a big spot that you contract to a small spot, and then cell fires,

00:13:11.859 --> 00:13:14.193
because you take away the inhibition.

00:13:14.194 --> 00:13:18.197
It's just showing the center and surround arrangement

00:13:18.198 --> 00:13:21.801
that Kuffler was the first to show in retinal ganglion cells,

00:13:21.802 --> 00:13:23.669
but this is a geniculate cell.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:27.773
Now, we go... just to show that this is

00:13:27.774 --> 00:13:29.942
a circular and symmetric kind of organization

00:13:29.943 --> 00:13:31.677
of the receptive field,

00:13:31.678 --> 00:13:33.913
and to show that we have this bar

00:13:33.914 --> 00:13:36.182
move across the receptive field

00:13:36.183 --> 00:13:38.050
in different orientations.

00:13:38.051 --> 00:13:41.454
And you can hear that

00:13:41.455 --> 00:13:43.923
there's no real difference in the frequency of the impulses from...

00:13:43.924 --> 00:13:46.459
we're still recording from a single nerve cell

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:48.895
in the relay nucleus.

00:13:48.896 --> 00:13:50.696
Now, we have suddenly a pitch change,

00:13:50.697 --> 00:13:53.099
and you can hear that,

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.768
and now we are recording from a single cell

00:13:55.769 --> 00:13:57.737
in the primary visual cortex.

00:13:57.738 --> 00:14:00.206
And we map out the light-sensitive area,

00:14:00.207 --> 00:14:02.642
the receptive field,

00:14:02.643 --> 00:14:04.877
of a single cortical cell.

00:14:04.878 --> 00:14:07.446
And you can see...

00:14:07.447 --> 00:14:09.482
the blackboard we had before...

00:14:09.483 --> 00:14:12.518
it's very simple

00:14:12.519 --> 00:14:14.387
and you can draw on this piece of paper the outline,

00:14:14.388 --> 00:14:17.790
and the size of the field

00:14:17.791 --> 00:14:20.560
is a little bit bigger than indicated here

00:14:20.561 --> 00:14:23.563
and the orientation is a little bit counterclockwise,

00:14:23.564 --> 00:14:25.831
but in principle this is actually

00:14:25.832 --> 00:14:29.735
an experiment that David and I carried out

00:14:29.736 --> 00:14:31.404
some 50-60 years ago.

00:14:31.405 --> 00:14:33.439
We just wanted to have it documented,

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.474
because at first people didn't believe us

00:14:35.475 --> 00:14:38.811
that it was true that cells had this property

00:14:38.812 --> 00:14:40.546
that you now would see.

00:14:40.547 --> 00:14:42.715
Some cells...

00:14:42.716 --> 00:14:45.384
this cell responded a little bit better to leftward movement than right,

00:14:45.385 --> 00:14:48.020
and some cells responded

00:14:48.021 --> 00:14:49.889
only to one direction,

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:55.394
and other cells prefer one over the other.

00:14:55.395 --> 00:15:00.266
So, this is the critical point coming up now.

00:15:00.267 --> 00:15:03.836
Keep in mind, we are stimulating the same visual area

00:15:03.837 --> 00:15:05.838
in the cortex,

00:15:05.839 --> 00:15:08.140
but this cell doesn't see a horizontal bar

00:15:08.141 --> 00:15:12.245
-- it can only see a bar along the vertical.

00:15:12.246 --> 00:15:16.983
So, this is nature's amazing picture,

00:15:16.984 --> 00:15:19.519
and they...

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:22.355
by asking and exploring nature,

00:15:22.356 --> 00:15:26.325
the brain told us about this trick.

00:15:26.326 --> 00:15:30.730
Next, we just show a simple paintbrush,

00:15:30.731 --> 00:15:34.634
just to show you how

00:15:34.635 --> 00:15:37.637
strong and simple this system is,

00:15:37.638 --> 00:15:45.511
to detect any orientation of any contour.

00:15:45.512 --> 00:15:48.447
So, the... I'd like to go back, now,

00:15:48.448 --> 00:15:50.616
to this diagram,

00:15:50.617 --> 00:15:53.085
just to point out that,

00:15:53.086 --> 00:15:56.322
within the receptive...

00:15:56.323 --> 00:16:01.761
the area of the receptive field, the light-sensitive area,

00:16:01.762 --> 00:16:09.368
the cell you just saw

00:16:09.369 --> 00:16:11.337
prefers sort of close to vertical orientation.

00:16:11.338 --> 00:16:14.807
Now, when David and I first discovered this...

00:16:14.808 --> 00:16:18.611
by chance, actually, it wasn't because we were clever...

00:16:18.612 --> 00:16:22.014
it just happened that the contour of a...

00:16:22.015 --> 00:16:24.684
of this orientation happened to pass by and we said,

00:16:24.685 --> 00:16:26.385
"Oh, what does this mean?"

00:16:26.386 --> 00:16:29.322
We couldn’t activate the cell with spots of light,

00:16:29.323 --> 00:16:32.024
so David, who was very...

00:16:32.025 --> 00:16:34.460
he rushed down the hall and called on our colleagues

00:16:34.461 --> 00:16:37.964
to come and say, "Look what we have found!"

00:16:37.965 --> 00:16:40.733
And so everybody came and were amazed, like we were,

00:16:40.734 --> 00:16:44.070
about the specificity with which this cell responded

00:16:44.071 --> 00:16:46.873
-- preferring one orientation over any other.

00:16:46.874 --> 00:16:49.976
But within this square, here,

00:16:49.977 --> 00:16:54.080
there are many...

00:16:54.081 --> 00:16:56.816
there are other neighboring cells who prefer a different orientation.

00:16:56.817 --> 00:17:00.152
In the drawing, here, we have a horizontal,

00:17:00.153 --> 00:17:02.288
and there are other weak orientations.

00:17:02.289 --> 00:17:05.491
Perhaps there is a representation of 20 different orientations,

00:17:05.492 --> 00:17:07.693
but the cells...

00:17:07.694 --> 00:17:10.796
all these cells see the same little area,

00:17:10.797 --> 00:17:13.499
so you have to think about our visual system

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:20.673
as partialized into small light-sensitive areas,

00:17:20.674 --> 00:17:23.009
and then it's not until you get into higher visual areas

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.077
when these areas come together,

00:17:25.078 --> 00:17:27.079
and you have bigger areas of light sensitivity,

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:30.349
so you can see the whole world.

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:34.053
But the initial step in the processing of visual information

00:17:34.054 --> 00:17:36.822
is this partialization of images.

00:17:36.823 --> 00:17:39.559
And the idea, here,

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:44.130
is that these cells sensitive for an orientation of a contour

00:17:44.131 --> 00:17:47.767
are the instrument used by the brain to visualize...

00:17:47.768 --> 00:17:49.802
to rec...

00:17:49.803 --> 00:17:53.372
to make it possible for us to perceive any object.

00:17:53.373 --> 00:17:59.212
This slide is from a book by the late David Marr,

00:17:59.213 --> 00:18:02.582
who was a...

00:18:02.583 --> 00:18:05.751
he liked the idea about...

00:18:05.752 --> 00:18:11.924
he was more a model builder, and he said that...

00:18:11.925 --> 00:18:14.794
if you look at a teddy bear,

00:18:14.795 --> 00:18:18.664
and you have these cells sensitive to orientation or contour,

00:18:18.665 --> 00:18:23.970
and he drew, here, to the right, the...

00:18:23.971 --> 00:18:28.374
here is a vertically oriented cell

00:18:28.375 --> 00:18:31.744
preferring the top of the head...

00:18:31.745 --> 00:18:35.481
so, you can actually draw the teddy bear

00:18:35.482 --> 00:18:37.817
using these very simple cells.

00:18:37.818 --> 00:18:47.326
So, that was the concept that...

00:18:47.327 --> 00:18:50.463
it's like having a Lego set in your camp,

00:18:50.464 --> 00:18:54.233
and you can look upon these cells as individual pieces,

00:18:54.234 --> 00:18:56.469
that then the brain can use

00:18:56.470 --> 00:18:59.305
in order to detect any object of interest.

00:18:59.306 --> 00:19:02.475
Now, the funny thing happened

00:19:02.476 --> 00:19:06.212
when we published this paper in the spring of 1969,

00:19:06.213 --> 00:19:11.551
not this figure but the fact that the visual...

00:19:11.552 --> 00:19:13.819
primary visual cortex is sensitive...

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:17.156
the cells there are sensitive to orientation of contours.

00:19:17.157 --> 00:19:21.360
I received a note, or we received a note,

00:19:21.361 --> 00:19:24.130
from John Z Young,

00:19:24.131 --> 00:19:27.333
who was a very prominent neurobiologist in Britain,

00:19:27.334 --> 00:19:30.536
and he said,

00:19:30.537 --> 00:19:34.273
"Torsten, this is very nice, but it's nothing new,"

00:19:34.274 --> 00:19:42.014
and then he sent me this picture of Van Gogh's self-portrait.

00:19:42.015 --> 00:19:44.317
So, you can... as you can see,

00:19:44.318 --> 00:19:48.221
Van Gogh gives oriented lines in order to draw his picture,

00:19:48.222 --> 00:19:50.990
and other artists have also used similar ways,

00:19:50.991 --> 00:19:54.560
and so you can ask,

00:19:54.561 --> 00:19:58.564
maybe artists have a more intuitive understanding of how our brain works,

00:19:58.565 --> 00:20:01.701
and they obviously don't realize it,

00:20:01.702 --> 00:20:04.537
and we don't either, necessarily,

00:20:04.538 --> 00:20:08.341
but this is one way, perhaps, that we should...

00:20:08.342 --> 00:20:11.377
like to bring the arts and sciences together,

00:20:11.378 --> 00:20:14.614
that by working together we may learn from each other.

00:20:14.615 --> 00:20:19.852
So, the ummm... so, this... this was ummm...

00:20:19.853 --> 00:20:22.989
the one discovery...

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:31.464
when we started out in September 1958,

00:20:31.465 --> 00:20:36.536
we discovered the orientational cell sensitivity...

00:20:36.537 --> 00:20:39.906
specificity of cortical cell orientation

00:20:39.907 --> 00:20:42.108
after three months,

00:20:42.109 --> 00:20:45.411
and so we quickly wrote a paper

00:20:45.412 --> 00:20:47.213
and then published in the spring.

00:20:47.214 --> 00:20:53.019
The point to come back to this slide is that...

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:56.556
to remind you that this discovery

00:20:56.557 --> 00:21:00.860
is critically important for our understanding how vision...

00:21:00.861 --> 00:21:03.696
how the visual information is coded,

00:21:03.697 --> 00:21:06.232
but done with very simple...

00:21:06.233 --> 00:21:09.435
two young guys using very simple tools.

00:21:09.436 --> 00:21:11.971
And that...

00:21:11.972 --> 00:21:14.006
like to inspire young people to,

00:21:14.007 --> 00:21:15.942
if you have an idea,

00:21:15.943 --> 00:21:18.244
just find out what you can do with it,

00:21:18.245 --> 00:21:21.514
and then go out and try it out

00:21:21.515 --> 00:21:23.950
and then once you find something that works,

00:21:23.951 --> 00:21:26.953
you can get the resources and become...

00:21:26.954 --> 00:21:31.057
build the proper laboratory, which we did.

00:21:31.058 --> 00:21:34.293
So, David and I continued to work.

00:21:34.294 --> 00:21:37.063
This, as I said, took a very short time,

00:21:37.064 --> 00:21:41.167
and later we spent 20 years together.

00:21:41.168 --> 00:21:48.541
David was supposed to come, as I mentioned, one month...

00:21:48.542 --> 00:21:53.179
one year while his lab in Mountcastle was renovated,

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:55.982
but we got along so well

00:21:55.983 --> 00:21:58.784
and so we stayed together and collaborated for 20 years,

00:21:58.785 --> 00:22:01.721
and during those 20 years we did...

00:22:01.722 --> 00:22:05.758
studied the cortical architecture,

00:22:05.759 --> 00:22:10.496
we studied... showed that the orient...

00:22:10.497 --> 00:22:13.165
cells with similar orientation

00:22:13.166 --> 00:22:15.635
are grouped in columnar organization

00:22:15.636 --> 00:22:17.703
in a very beautiful way,

00:22:17.704 --> 00:22:20.206
and that the two eyes work together in a very precise way,

00:22:20.207 --> 00:22:22.308
also in a columnar fashion.

00:22:22.309 --> 00:22:25.011
And we could show, also, that the development of the visual system,

00:22:25.012 --> 00:22:27.547
what we are both with

00:22:27.548 --> 00:22:29.348
and how that can be modified by the environment.

00:22:29.349 --> 00:22:31.584
So, this is along story,

00:22:31.585 --> 00:22:38.891
but the fact that we had the resources and time,

00:22:38.892 --> 00:22:41.794
but it just started out very simply...

00:22:41.795 --> 00:22:44.830
I think this is... we'll remember.

00:22:44.831 --> 00:22:49.368
Of course, we then, in 1981,

00:22:49.369 --> 00:22:52.038
we were told that we had won the Nobel Prize,

00:22:52.039 --> 00:22:56.075
so this is just a press conference in that context

00:22:56.076 --> 00:22:59.278
and we both looked happy.

00:22:59.279 --> 00:23:08.754
Now, David continued to stay at Harvard until 2013,

00:23:08.755 --> 00:23:13.626
when he died,

00:23:13.627 --> 00:23:18.998
and I still feel strongly that...

00:23:18.999 --> 00:23:21.334
I used to call...

00:23:21.335 --> 00:23:25.004
we used to call each other scientific brothers, and that's true.

00:23:25.005 --> 00:23:28.441
We became, scientifically, very close

00:23:28.442 --> 00:23:32.078
and enjoyed each other scientifically,

00:23:32.079 --> 00:23:36.115
whereas at the same time we had our own personal lives.

00:23:36.116 --> 00:23:41.687
But it was a real brotherhood-type...

00:23:41.688 --> 00:23:44.657
scientific brotherhood situation.

00:23:44.658 --> 00:23:47.426
So, I still miss the guy.

00:23:47.427 --> 00:24:04.211
So, thank you.

